Great question! Here are the specific permissions/roles the mobile app service account needs:

## **Required Roles for Mobile Service Account**

### **Core Permissions:**
```
Cloud Run Invoker
- roles/run.invoker
- Needed to call the Cloud Run API endpoints

Firestore User 
- roles/datastore.user
- Read/write access to Firestore (for device registration, session management)
```

### **Optional (if using other Google services):**
```
Storage Object Admin (if using Cloud Storage for file uploads)
- roles/storage.objectAdmin
- Only if storing photos/signatures in Cloud Storage

Service Account Token Creator (if impersonating other accounts)
- roles/iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator 
- Usually not needed for mobile apps
```

## **Minimal Production Setup:**

**For your mobile app service account:**
```bash
# Create the service account
gcloud iam service-accounts create mobile-app-client \
 --display-name="Mobile App Client" \
 --description="Service account for EasyDelivery mobile applications"

# Grant Cloud Run access
gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding easydelivery-433910 \
 --member="serviceAccount:mobile-app-client@easydelivery-433910.iam.gserviceaccount.com" \
 --role="roles/run.invoker"

# Grant Firestore access 
gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding easydelivery-433910 \
 --member="serviceAccount:mobile-app-client@easydelivery-433910.iam.gserviceaccount.com" \
 --role="roles/datastore.user"

# Generate the key
gcloud iam service-accounts keys create mobile-app-key.json \
 --iam-account=mobile-app-client@easydelivery-433910.iam.gserviceaccount.com
```

## **Security Best Practices:**

### **Firestore Security Rules:**
Since the mobile app will have Firestore access, ensure your Firestore rules restrict access appropriately:

```javascript
rules_version = '2';
service cloud.firestore {
 match /databases/{database}/documents {
 // Only allow access to specific collections
 match /devices/{deviceId} {
 allow read, write: if request.auth != null;
 }

 match /sessions/{sessionId} {
 allow read, write: if request.auth != null;
 }

 match /trips/{tripId} {
 allow read: if request.auth != null;
 }

 // Deny access to sensitive collections
 match /clients/{clientId} {
 allow read: if false; // API handles this
 }
 }
}
```

### **Why These Permissions:**

1. **`roles/run.invoker`**: Allows the mobile app to call your Cloud Run API endpoints
2. **`roles/datastore.user`**: Allows direct Firestore access for offline capabilities and caching
3. **No admin roles**: Mobile apps should never have admin access to production resources

### **Alternative Approach (More Secure):**
If you want maximum security, you could use **only `roles/run.invoker`** and have the Cloud Run API handle ALL Firestore operations. This means:
- Mobile app can only call API endpoints
- No direct Firestore access from mobile
- All data access goes through your API (better audit trail)

**Which approach would you prefer?**
1. **Direct Firestore access** (faster, offline-capable, requires Firestore rules)
2. **API-only access** (more secure, easier to audit, requires internet)

I'd recommend starting with **API-only access** for production security, then adding direct Firestore access later if you need offline capabilities.
